      "articleId": "aHR0cHM6Ly9qYXBhbi5jbmV0LmNvbS9yZWxlYXNlLzMxMjIyNDc4Lw==",
      "headline": "【横浜市立大学】風疹ウイルスの主要侵入受容体Nectin-4を同定",
      "headlineTranslated": "Yokohama City University Identifies Nectin-4 as the Major Entry Receptor for Rubella Virus.",
      "summary": "Rubella virus uses Nectin-4 as the major host cell entry receptor, a finding by a Yokohama City University–led team published in Nature (November 2026). Using rubella virus–like particles and CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens, they show Nectin-4 mediates entry in airway epithelial cells and placental syncytiotrophoblasts. In air–liquid interface airway models, rubella infects from the basal surface and releases progeny apically; infection is markedly reduced by Nectin-4 inhibition. Placental organoids reveal high Nectin-4 on syncytiotrophoblast surfaces, with infection suppressed by Nectin-4–blocking antibodies. Notably, Nectin-4 is also a measles virus receptor, suggesting shared host factors across these distinct viruses. This discovery advances understanding of rubella airway shedding and maternal–fetal transmission and may inform antiviral or vaccine strategies against congenital rubella syndrome. Background: global congenital rubella cases around 30,000/year (2019).",
      "summaryOriginal": "Rubella virus uses Nectin-4 as the major host cell entry receptor, a finding by a Yokohama City University–led team published in Nature (November 2026). Using rubella virus–like particles and CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens, they show Nectin-4 mediates entry in airway epithelial cells and placental syncytiotrophoblasts. In air–liquid interface airway models, rubella infects from the basal surface and releases progeny apically; infection is markedly reduced by Nectin-4 inhibition. Placental organoids reveal high Nectin-4 on syncytiotrophoblast surfaces, with infection suppressed by Nectin-4–blocking antibodies. Notably, Nectin-4 is also a measles virus receptor, suggesting shared host factors across these distinct viruses. This discovery advances understanding of rubella airway shedding and maternal–fetal transmission and may inform antiviral or vaccine strategies against congenital rubella syndrome. Background: global congenital rubella cases around 30,000/year (2019).",
      "publishedTimestamp": "2026-11-09T05:35:54+00:00",
